<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khosrow I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/531">531</a>–579 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Anushirvan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khosrow I, also known as Anushirvan, was a Sasanian king who reigned in the sixth century CE and is remembered as one of the dynasty's greatest rulers. He reformed taxation and the army, strengthening central control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. His reign was also noted for patronage of learning and for wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and later Persian tradition idealised him as a just king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-i.jpg" alt="Khosrow I" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_the_Sasanian_king_Khosrow_I_Anushirvan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They made and unmade Sasanian kings, raising Peroz I in 458 and Kavad I in 488 and 498, and from 474 the Sasanian Empire paid them tribute until <b>Khosrow I</b>'s rise in the 530s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Khosrow I</b> Anushirvan reorganised the army on a more professional footing, dividing high command among four regional generals (spahbed) and paying and equipping cavalrymen from the reformed treasury, and in alliance with the Göktürk Khaganate destroyed the Hephthalite empire (c. 557–560 CE) that had long threatened the north-eastern…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Persian king <b>Khosrow I</b> refused to let them sell silk in Persia, the Sogdian Maniakh persuaded the Turkic ruler Istämi to seek trade with Byzantium, and in 568 he led an embassy to the emperor Justin II in Constantinople; the Byzantine envoy Zemarchus set out for…</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Khosrow I</b>, known as Anushirvan, succeeded his father Kavad I as Sasanian King of Kings and went on to negotiate an end to the Iberian War with the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Ibn Isfandiyar's history of Tabaristan, <b>Khosrow I</b> granted the descendants of Sukhra land in the mountains of Tabaristan around 550, after their service against the Hephthalites.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarinvand-dynasty">Qarinvand dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 570 the Sasanian king <b>Khosrow I</b> sent a small force under the general Wahriz at the request of Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan to expel the Ethiopians of Aksumite Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/state/sasanian-yemen">Sasanian Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ardestan, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.38,52.37&amp;year=555" aria-label="Ardestan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5889 952 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Khosrow I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6339 1222h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6339 1222h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.38,52.37&amp;year=555">What was here in 555 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Not far from the truth, I think, is the ancient saying that God does not give blessings unmixed, but He mingles them with troubles and then bestows them upon men.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Roman envoys after the capture of Antioch, 540 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 2) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1914 &#183; Procopius' account, which calls the words high-sounding &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16764/pg16764-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But when I behold the massacre of such a multitude of men, and the victory thus drenched with blood, there arises in me no sense of the delight that should follow my achievement.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Roman envoys after the capture of Antioch, 540 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 2) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1914 &#183; Procopius' account, which calls the words high-sounding &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16764/pg16764-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…but the Romans will have the Persians as their own soldiers for the future, dispensing to them a fixed payment for their service…&#8221;</p><footer>To the Roman envoys, demanding a yearly payment, 540 (Procopius, History of the Wars, book 2) &#183; tr. H. B. Dewing, 1914 &#183; Procopius' account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16764/pg16764-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
